  "deltas": {
    "active_source_issues": [],
    "decisions": [
      {
        "action": "replace",
        "decision_id": "D000988",
        "disposition": "existing_equivalent",
        "rationale": "Stacks now defines morphisms between graded connecting functors having the same arbitrary interval, variance, and step epsilon = +1 or -1 as componentwise natural transformations commuting with the correctly oriented boundary square in both the covariant and contravariant cases. Identities, sums, and composites are componentwise, and no exactness or one-sided-degree hypothesis is imposed. This gives the full p. 140 notion; the earlier nonnegative exact delta-functor definition is only a specialization.",
        "review_state": "integrated_source_stacks_and_independent_review",
        "stacks_labels": "homology-definition-morphism-graded-connecting-functors",
        "supersedes": "D000152",
        "unit_id": "tohoku:def:delta-functor-morphism"
      },
      {
        "action": "replace",
        "decision_id": "D000989",
        "disposition": "existing_equivalent",
        "rationale": "After the resolved p. 140 correction reads all five unbound i_0 occurrences as degree 0, Stacks defines universality on any interval containing 0 and for either variance and step. For step +1 every degree-zero map T^0 -> T'^0 extends uniquely to T -> T'; for step -1 both arrows reverse, so T'^0 -> T^0 extends uniquely to T' -> T. The interval may be finite and neither the universal object nor its competitors need be exact, making the finite degrees 0 through a definition an exact specialization.",
        "review_state": "integrated_source_stacks_and_independent_review",
        "stacks_labels": "homology-definition-universal-graded-connecting-functor",
        "supersedes": "D000164",
        "unit_id": "tohoku:def:universal-delta-functor"
      },
      {
        "action": "replace",
        "decision_id": "D000990",
        "disposition": "existing_equivalent",
        "rationale": "Stacks proves for any fixed interval containing degree 0, variance, and step that two universal graded connecting functors with fixed identifications of their degree-zero functors are joined by a unique isomorphism inducing the prescribed degree-zero identification. Specializing to covariant step +1 and degrees 0 through a, with a > 0, gives exactly the p. 140 satellite-uniqueness assertion. No existence of satellites, unqualified uniqueness under nontrivial automorphisms of F, or compatibility between different truncation lengths is claimed.",
        "review_state": "integrated_source_stacks_and_independent_review",
        "stacks_labels": "homology-lemma-uniqueness-universal-graded-connecting-functor",
        "supersedes": "D000198",
        "unit_id": "tohoku:prop:satellites-uniqueness"
      }
    ],
